From b0500daeb074214b1f0fab361f186f1405f8ff78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 17:28:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Replace the custom glass video controls with the browser's
 native ones

MediaVideo rendered its own play/pause, mute and fullscreen bar out of
GlassSurface buttons, gated behind hover/tap reveal logic. Swap all of
that for the standard `<video controls>` bar: drop the button UI, the
playing/muted/fullscreen state that only drove its icons, the
containerRef-based fullscreen wrapper, and the `chrome` prop that
selected between hover-reveal and always-on.

Two behaviors the custom bar used to provide are kept, adapted to work
without it:

- Only one video plays audio at a time. The cross-mute broadcast used
  to fire from the custom mute button's click handler; it now fires
  from the `volumechange` listener instead, so it fires the same way
  whether a viewer unmutes through the native control or otherwise.
- A video the viewer paused on purpose doesn't resume when it scrolls
  back into view, even though scrolling it out of view also stops
  playback (via `load()`, to free the decoder budget). Previously the
  custom pause button set a ref directly; now the native `pause` event
  covers both cases indistinguishably, so a ref is set immediately
  before the deactivation-triggered `load()` and consumed by the next
  `pause` event, leaving only a genuine viewer pause to set it.

MediaImage's `controls` prop drops its `'hover'` variant (native
controls have their own built-in show/hide behavior) and becomes a
plain boolean; callers in media-block.tsx and photo-gallery.tsx updated
to match.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01X9ijuMVxdyw9LcC5DjUmXo
